    "title": "A rare glimpse of the northern lights and a warm stretch of weather too - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Bryan Hale No media credit provided. ,\n2 hours ago\nHARLINGEN, Texas ( ValleyCentral ) —Now through 2 am, overnight tonight, you might be able to catch a faint glimpse of the iconic northern lights.\nThe phenomenon is an incredible light show in the atmosphere, more accurately known as the Aurora Borealis. Sometimes the sun generates huge solar storms that burp out charged particles streaming through space toward Earth. Those particles collide with the atmosphere and produce an incredible, colorful light display.\nIt is quite rare to see the lights even as far south as Dallas, but tonight a fairly large solar episode is approaching the planet. If everything goes right, there may be an opportunity to see the lights from the northern fringes of the Rio Grande Valley.\nBe sure to put yourself a few miles from any town or city, as the light pollution will ruin your chances.\nOn a more meteorological note, the Valley will remain under the influence of high pressure, leading to lots of sun and southeast breezes each day, sending the mercury well above normal and snuffing out any chance for rain well into next week.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KVEO-TV.",
